iov[0] → first string
iov[1] → second string
iov[2] → third string

So the “iovec vector of 3 buffers” just means an array of 3 separate strings ready to write.

Normally, if you wanted to write these strings to a file with write(), you’d do:

write(fd, buf[0], strlen(buf[0]));
write(fd, buf[1], strlen(buf[1]));
write(fd, buf[2], strlen(buf[2]));
Three separate system calls → more overhead
Each call could be interrupted or interleaved with another process

With writev(fd, iov, 3):

All 3 buffers are written in one single system call
Atomic write: no other process can insert data between them
Efficient: avoids multiple calls and copying

