“Linux implements readv() and writev() as system calls, and internally performs scatter/gather I/O”
Meaning:

When you call writev(fd, iov, 3) in your C program:

Your program makes a system call to the kernel.
The kernel knows the iov array and handles copying all buffers to the file efficiently in one operation.
This is called scatter/gather I/O, because multiple memory segments (scatter) are gathered and written to one file in one atomic operation.
Key point: The kernel is already optimized for this. You don’t need to loop over buffers yourself — it’s faster and safer.
2️⃣ “In fact, all I/O inside the Linux kernel is vectored; read() and write() are implemented as vectored I/O with a vector of only one segment”

Meaning:
The normal read(fd, buf, n) and write(fd, buf, n) are actually special cases of readv/writev, where the vector has only 1 element.
So:

